# Contact-Center Volume Forecast and Staffing Plan

**Business question:** How many agents are needed per 30-minute interval over the next 13 weeks to answer 80% of calls within 60 seconds, and how much does the planned February price-change notice add?

![13-week forecast with backtest and price-notice scenario](figures/01_headline_forecast.png)

## TL;DR

- **The forecast beats a seasonal-naive baseline by {wape_cut}%.** WAPE is {wape_reg}% vs. {wape_naive}% (MASE {mase_reg} vs. {mase_naive}), measured over 8 rolling origins with 13-week horizons. The 80% prediction intervals covered {pi_cov}% of actual days.
- **Q1 2026 needs {req_mean} FTE on average against a roster of {roster}.** The roster falls short in {gap_weeks} of {full_weeks} full weeks, starting the week of {first_gap}. With 6 weeks of training, the hire-by date was {hire_by}. **Recommendation: hire {hires} agents now (productive from {ready}) and cover the gap with overtime.**
- **The Feb 2 price notice adds about {notice_extra} calls over 10 days** and pushes the requirement to {scen_peak} FTE in the week of {scen_peak_week} (+{notice_fte} FTE). The overtime budget is {ot_scen} for the quarter, of which {ot_notice} ({ot_notice_h} hours at $38) is for the notice.
- **Service level has been below the 80% target every week of 2025** ({sl_2025}% answered within 60 s; {ab_2025}% abandoned). The schedule did not keep up with {trend}% monthly growth.

> **Synthetic data notice.** All data in this repository is synthetic, generated by `src/generate_data.py` (seed 42). No real patients, members, customers, or organizations. Findings describe the simulated system, not any real company.

## Business context (simulated)

The Director of Customer Care at a simulated regional home-internet provider has to approve the Q1 2026 hiring plan: how many FTE, hired by when given a 6-week training lead time. The director also has to set an overtime budget for the surge after the February price-change notice. The center runs five queues (billing, tech support, moves/new service, cancellations, outage line), 07:00-21:00 Central, 7 days a week. The service-level goal is 80% of calls answered within 60 seconds.

## Skills demonstrated

- Time-series forecasting: seasonal naive, Holt-Winters ETS, and regression with event regressors and AR(1) errors (statsmodels SARIMAX)
- Honest evaluation: rolling-origin backtest (8 folds x 13 weeks), WAPE and MASE against seasonal naive, 80% prediction-interval coverage
- Event and scenario modeling: an event study with a fitted decay curve, outage elasticity, billing-cycle effects
- Capacity planning: Erlang C written from scratch ([`src/erlang.py`](src/erlang.py)), interval-level agent requirements, shrinkage, FTE, hiring date, overtime cost
- SQL (DuckDB, written to port to PostgreSQL): `AT TIME ZONE` conversions, window functions, range joins, a `VALUES` mapping table, calendar marts
- Data cleaning: mixed UTC/local timestamps, DST artifacts, renamed queue codes, missing days, contacts vs. legs

## Approach

1. **Generate** seven tables ({n_rows_legs} call legs, {n_rows_staff} staffing intervals, 120,000 customers, 90 outages) with planted effects and data-quality issues: [`src/generate_data.py`](src/generate_data.py).
2. **Load** into DuckDB: [`src/load.py`](src/load.py).
3. **Stage and clean**: [`sql/01_staging/`](sql/01_staging/). This step fixes time zones, maps queue codes, zeroes handle time on abandons, and collapses DST duplicates.
4. **Model marts**: [`sql/02_marts/`](sql/02_marts/). This step builds contacts (demand) vs. legs (workload), a daily feature calendar, and day-by-queue and interval-by-queue facts.
5. **Answer the questions**: [`sql/03_analysis/`](sql/03_analysis/) (interval profile, billing cycle, outage elasticity, price-notice event study, service-level history, staffing requirement).
6. **Model and size** in Python: [`src/analysis.py`](src/analysis.py) runs the effect recovery, backtest, Q1 2026 forecast, and Erlang C staffing, and writes `reports/results.json`.
7. **Report**: [`src/figures.py`](src/figures.py), [`src/dashboard.py`](src/dashboard.py) produces [`dashboard/index.html`](dashboard/index.html), and [`src/report.py`](src/report.py) fills this README from `results.json`.

**Contacts vs. legs.** A contact is one customer call. A transfer adds a second leg in another queue ({multi}% of contacts). Demand is counted in contacts ({contacts}). Workload and staffing are counted in legs ({legs}), because each leg takes an agent in its queue. The daily forecasts and Erlang C inputs use legs.

## Key findings

### 1. The regression forecast beats both baselines in every queue

![Backtest accuracy](figures/03_backtest_accuracy.png)

| Model | WAPE (all queues) | MASE |
|---|---|---|
| Seasonal naive (repeat last week) | {wape_naive}% | {mase_naive} |
| Holt-Winters ETS (damped trend, weekly seasonality) | {wape_ets}% | {mase_ets} |
| Regression: trend, weekday, holidays, billing cycle, promos, price notice, expected outage load; AR(1) errors | **{wape_reg}%** | **{mase_reg}** |

The backtest uses 8 origins from {first_origin} to {last_origin}, 4 weeks apart, each forecasting 13 weeks. Days that are closed or missing are not scored. Outage load uses its historical average (outages are not known in advance), so no future information leaks into the backtest. Accuracy is best for {best_queue} ({best_queue_wape}%) and worst for {worst_queue} ({worst_queue_wape}%), the smallest queue.

### 2. Seasonality, billing cycles, and outages drive intraday and daily volume

![Intraday profile](figures/06_intraday_profile.png)

- **Weekday:** Monday runs {mon}x Thursday and Sunday {sun}x (Poisson regression with trend and events).
- **Intraday:** the 10:00-11:30 intervals run {peak1}x the interval mean and 13:00-14:30 runs {peak2}x.
- **Billing cycle:** billing-queue contacts rise {bill}% (95% CI {bill_lo}% to {bill_hi}%) on days 1-3 after each bill date (the 1st and 15th).
- **Outages:** each outage adds {outage_k} tech-support calls per 1,000 affected customers in the first 4 hours (95% CI {outage_lo} to {outage_hi}; {outage_n} outages).

![Outage elasticity](figures/05_outage_elasticity.png)

### 3. The 2025 price notice: +{pn_lift}% calls on day 0, half-life {pn_hl} days, and longer calls

![Event study](figures/04_price_notice_event_study.png)

The counterfactual comes from Poisson regressions for billing and cancellations, fitted on 2025 outside the response window, with trend, weekday, billing-cycle, and holiday terms. The notice is a Monday that falls just before billing-cycle days, so a same-weekday baseline would mix the two effects. The fitted decay is +{pn_lift}% on day 0 (95% CI {pn_lo}% to {pn_hi}%) with a {pn_hl}-day half-life (95% CI {pn_hl_lo} to {pn_hl_hi}), for about {pn_excess} excess contacts over 10 days. Average handle time on those queues rose {aht_lift}% for 14 days. Service level in the notice week fell to {sl_notice}%. The 2026 scenario reuses these estimates.

### 4. Staffing: hire {hires} now and budget overtime for the notice

![Weekly FTE vs roster](figures/02_weekly_fte_vs_roster.png)

The daily forecast is spread across intervals using each queue's weekday profile from the last 26 weeks. Each interval x queue gets Erlang C agents for 80/60 at the Q4 2025 average handle time (billing {aht_bill} min, tech {aht_tech} min). FTE = productive agent-hours / (1 - 32% shrinkage) / 40.

| | Baseline | With Feb 2 price notice |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 2026 calls (legs) | {q1_base} | {q1_scen} |
| Peak weekly requirement | {req_peak} FTE | {scen_peak} FTE |
| Weeks over the {roster}-FTE roster | {gap_weeks} of {full_weeks} | {gap_weeks} of {full_weeks} |
| Overtime, with {hires} hires productive from {ready} | {ot_base} | {ot_scen} |
| Overtime with no hiring | {ot_nohire_base} | {ot_nohire_scen} |

Baseline volume is {growth}% above Q1 2025. If staffing to the P90 volume (upper 80% PI) instead of the mean, the peak baseline requirement is {req_p90} FTE.

### 5. Service level has been below target, and five small queues cost capacity

![Service-level history](figures/07_service_level_history.png)

The 2025 service level was {sl_2025}% ({sl_weeks_ok} of {sl_weeks} weeks at or above 80%), falling to {sl_q4}% in Q4. Occupancy averaged {occ}%, which is low because each of five small queues needs its own Erlang C buffer. If one cross-trained team answered all queues, Erlang C would need {pooled_fte} FTE instead of {req_mean}, about {pooled_save} FTE less. That is an upper bound: it ignores cross-training cost and any AHT increase.

## Recommendations

1. **Open {hires} requisitions now** (Finding 4). The 6-week hire-by date ({hire_by}) has already passed, so new hires are productive from {ready} at the earliest.
2. **Approve an overtime budget of {ot_scen} for Q1**, including {ot_notice} for the week of {scen_peak_week} and the week after (Findings 3 and 4). Schedule the overtime in the 10:00-11:30 and 13:00-14:30 peaks on Feb 2-6, when billing and cancellation calls run highest.
3. **Pilot cross-training between billing and cancellations, the two queues the price notice hits** (Finding 5). Pooling cuts the Erlang C buffer. Re-run this model monthly with the latest actuals, and track WAPE against the seasonal-naive benchmark.

